Print only whole-word matches with -w -o, and no matches with -o -v.
Matching with -w retries the regex after the end of each candidate that
is not a whole word, starting at that offset in the line, so the match
it accepts has offsets relative to that later position. The -o loop
read them as offsets from the start of the line and printed the wrong
text: "foobar foo" printed "ar " instead of "foo". Its later searches
then printed every match, so "foo foobar" printed "foo" twice.

A line selected by -v has no match, but the -o loop still ran on the
offsets of the last candidate that -w or -x rejected, and printed a
file name or line number prefix for it and for each match after it:
"foobar" printed "1:" with grep -n -v -x -o foo.

Make the accepted offsets relative to the line, apply the whole-word
test to each later match in the -o loop, and skip the loop when the
line has no match, as -F -o -v already does.

